{
  "dom_id": "step-q42075",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{11}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*",
  "worked_solution": "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{11}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-(-2)) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
